\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

C++ currently provides no means of exponentiating integers or
computing integer logarithms.
These are basic and useful operations that would be a good fit for standardization.

\h2{Motivation}

Integer exponentiation and logarithms are fundamental operations
that many programming languages provide,
sometimes as a builtin operator such as \tcode{**}.
A standard library operation for exponentiation would provide a wide range of capabilities:
\ul{
  \li{
    Exponentiation is necessary for a wide range of statistical computations.
    Most obviously, given and independent choice between \math{\mi{k}} options
    repeated \math{\mi{n}} times,
    the total number of combinations is \math{\msup{\mi{k}\mi{n}}},
    i.e. \tcode{std::ipow(k, n)}.
  }
  \li{
    Exponentiation is useful when performing calculations with units.
    For example, if
    kilo is \math{\mn{1000}},
    mega is \math{\msup{\mn{1000}\mn{2}}}, and so forth,
    applying that unit can be done by multiplying
    with \tcode{std::ipow(1000, n)} or with \tcode{std::ipow10(10, n * 3)}.
    Conversely, the base-10 logarithm can be used to determine which unit
    should be displayed when printing a number.
  }
  \li{
    Exponentiation allows for avoiding \q{magic numbers} and expressing intent more clearly,
    such as by writing \tcode{std::ipow10<int>(6)} instead of \tcode{1'000'000}
    or \tcode{std::ipow(5, 5)} instead of \tcode{3125}.
  }
  \li{
    Exponentiation provides a convenient inline notation.
    Instead of writing \tcode{(x + y) * (x + y)},
    one can write \tcode{std::ipow(x + y, 2)}.
  }
}

Beyond the mere utility,
exponentiation and logarithms should be standardized
because the ad-hoc solutions users provide may be inefficient.
The key insights are that:
\ul{
  \li{
    Integer exponentiation with a runtime base base can be performed using
    \ref("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponentiation_by_squaring"){exponentiation by squaring}
    rather than with repeated multiplication,
    which requires logarithmic time complexity rather than linear time complexity.
  }
  \li{
    Integer exponentiation with a known base can be performed in constant time
    using a lookup table.
  }
  \li{
    Integer logarithms with a runtime base can be performed using
    repeated multiplication to see which power of the base
    is greater or equal to the given number.
    An implementation using repeated division may be much slower.
  }
  \li{
    Integer logarithms with a known base can be performed in constant time on modern hardware
    by computing the base-2 logarithm via \tcode{std::countl_zero}
    and converting that to a base-n logarithm.
  }
}

\h2{Design}

The following function templates are proposed:

\cppblock{
// in <numeric>
template<class T> constexpr T ipow(T base, int exp);
template<class T> constexpr T ipow10(int exp);

template<class T> constexpr int ilog(T x);
template<class T> constexpr int ilog2(T x);
template<class T> constexpr int ilog10(T x);
}

… where \tcode{T} is constrained to be a signed or unsigned integer type.

\dl{
  \dt{Why not accept any integral type as \tcode{T}?}
  \dd{
    Modern standard library design such as in \eelis{numeric.sat} and \eelis{bit}
    uses a constraint that allows only signed or unsigned integer types.
  }

  \dt{What happens when you compute the logarithm of zero or less?}
  \dd{
    The behavior is undefined.
    This is consistent with error handling in other low-level numeric utilities.
  }

  \dt{What happens when the result doesn't fit in the return type?}
  \dd{
    The behavior is undefined.
  }

  \dt{Why is \tcode{int} used for exponents and logarithm return types?}
  \dd{
    \tcode{int} is typically used in numeric utilities when possible,
    such as in \tcode{int std::popcount} or \tcode{std::numeric_limits<T>::digits}.
    Having an additional template parameter would just result in extra instantiations.
  }

  \dt{Why happens when you pass in a negative exponent?}
  \dd{
    Zero is returned, which is a mathematical consequence of
    \tcode{std::ipow(n, e)} being defined as
    \math{\mrow{\mo{⌊}\msup{\mi{n}\mi{e}}\mo{⌋}}}.
  }

  \dt{Doesn't the use of \tcode{int} in \tcode{std::ipow} impose extra cost to check for negatives?}
  \dd{
    In theory yes.
    In practice, \tcode{std::ipow10} would always be inlined,
    and if the exponent is known to be positive at compile time, the check would be omitted.
    \tcode{std::ipow} may not always be inlined,
    but the branch on negative exponents is easily predictable, very cheap,
    and relatively small compared to the overall cost of the function.
  }

  \dt{Why is there no \tcode{std::ipow2}?}
  \dd{
    \tcode{std::ipow2<T>(exp)} would be equivalent to \tcode{T{1} << exp},
    and there is no reason to ever use it.
  }

  \dt{Why do we even need \tcode{std::ipow10}, \tcode{std::ilog2}, and \tcode{std::ilog10}?}
  \dd{
    As explained above,
    known bases allow for faster constant-time algorithms.
    The bases 2 and 10 are particularly useful.
  }

  \dt{Why \header{numeric}?}
  \dd{
    This is where other C++-specific numeric utilities go,
    such as saturation arithmetic, \tcode{std::gcd}, etc.
  }
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

Add a feature-test macro to \eelis{version.syn} as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_ipow 20XXXXL // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<numeric>}}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[numeric.ops.ipow]}

Immediately following \eelis{numeric.ops.midpoint},
insert a new subclause:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Integer exponentiation and logarithm \stable_ref{numeric.ops.ipow}}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr T ipow(T b, int e);
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

\pnum
\expects
\tcode{(b != 0 || e != 0)} is \tcode{true}
and the result is representable in \tcode{T}.

\pnum
\returns
\math{\msup{\mi{b}\mi{e}}} with the fractional part (if any) discarded.

\pnum
\remarks
A function call expression
that violates the precondition in the \Fundescx{Preconditions} element
is not a core constant expression\iref{expr.const.core}.
}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr T ipow10(int exp);
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

\pnum
\mandates
The value 10 is representable in \tcode{T}.

\pnum
\effects
Equivalent to:
\tcode{return ipow(T(10), exp);}
}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr int ilog(T x, T b);
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

\pnum
\expects
\tcode{(x > 0 && b > 1)} is \tcode{true}.

\pnum
\returns
\math{\mrow{\msub{\mo{log}\mi{b}}\mi{x}}} with the fractional part (if any) discarded.

\pnum
\remarks
A function call expression
that violates the precondition in the \Fundescx{Preconditions} element
is not a core constant expression\iref{expr.const.core}.
}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr int ilog2(T x);
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

\pnum
\mandates
The value 2 is representable in \tcode{T}.

\pnum
\effects
Equivalent to:
\tcode{return ilog(x, T(2));}
}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr int ilog10(T x);
}

\itemdescr{
\pnum
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

\pnum
\mandates
The value 10 is representable in \tcode{T}.

\pnum
\effects
Equivalent to:
\tcode{return ilog(x, T(10));}
}
}

